{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And the inheritance of the children of Israel shall not turn from tribe to tribe</b> – Scripture was concerned only to remedy that situation, for if there were in Israel women married to another tribe and they were inheriting an inheritance today, or if they would inherit from this day forward when their father or their brother dies without sons, perforce their inheritance would turn from tribe to tribe. And who could rectify at that time what had already been distorted? For the Torah did not wish to command that their son and husband not inherit them, for it did not see fit to uproot the law of inheritance. And likewise Scripture is not concerned about future eventualities, for daughters who are not inheriting an inheritance may marry into any of the tribes, and it is possible that they may become heirs to an inheritance in the future, for their brothers may die during the father's lifetime and the inheritance of their father or their kinsmen would pass to them. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Bava Batra 112a) who expounded: this matter applies only to that generation alone, it is possible that in all of that generation coming into the Land there were no daughters inheriting an inheritance other than the daughters of Tzelophehad, for therefore they alone spoke. And Scripture commanded that if a man dies from that day until the Land is divided among their tribes, and his daughter inherits him, she shall not marry into another tribe, so that that man should not come at the time of the division of the Land to take for himself an inheritance within another tribe, for the concern for them would be greater at the time of the division — that the tribes not intermingle with one another in inheritance — for afterward their inheritance is already known and they would not be so concerned. And because the time of the division was not known, He warned the entire generation. And HaShem, in whose hand is the soul of all the living, has no cause for concern from that point onward.\n\nAnd it is correct according to the plain sense that 'and the inheritance shall not turn' is not the reason in order that the inheritance not turn, but rather they are two commandments: He commanded regarding those who inherit an inheritance that they not marry except into the tribe of their father, and He commanded regarding those already married to whom an inheritance would come after they had a husband, that the inheritance not turn from tribe to another tribe, but rather their brothers and kinsmen shall inherit them — not their sons and not their husbands. And He warned first concerning the daughters of Tzelophehad, and afterward concerning every daughter who inherits an inheritance. And He stated concerning all Israel that the inheritance shall not turn from tribe to tribe, so that even those who are married or those who have been warned, if they transgress the commandment, the men of the tribe shall inherit them, so that they shall be joined and bound to the inheritance of the tribe of their fathers — their inheritance shall not be separated from them and they shall not be separated from the inheritance of their fathers. And all this applied only to the generation that inherited the Land at the time of the division, and behold He gave them counsel regarding the unmarried ones who asked, and He also provided a remedy for the married ones." "The Book of the Countings and the Banners of the Hosts is completed. And to the Elohim of Hosts, praise and thanksgivings, by hundreds and thousands and myriads. And just as He performed for our forefathers great and awesome deeds, may He hasten in our days the end of wonders, may He rebuild the House and its chambers, and the boundary before the rooms, and may He prepare there thrones for the House of David, and may our eyes behold it. And may He in His mercy purify us from hidden sins and from all errors, and may He show us wonders from His Torah. And may this indeed be His will, Amen."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "The citation (בבא בתרא קכ) refers to Bava Batra 120a in standard pagination; the folio designation ק״כ = 120 was resolved as 120a based on the well-known passage there."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:41.656438679Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}